      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by expressing the complex number \\( z \\) in its standard form \\( z = x + yi \\), where \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are real numbers and \\( i \\) is the imaginary unit. Given the equation \\( z^2 = 4z - 19 + 8i \\), we can substitute \\( z = x + yi \\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n(x + yi)^2 = 4(x + yi) - 19 + 8i.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 2xyi + (yi)^2 = x^2 + 2xyi - y^2 = (x^2 - y^2) + 2xyi.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right side, we get:\n\n\\[\n4x + 4yi - 19 + 8i = (4x - 19) + (4y + 8)i.\n\\]\n\nEquating the real and imaginary parts from both sides of the equation, we obtain two equations:\n\n1. \\( x^2 - y^2 = 4x - 19 \\),\n2. \\( 2xy = 4y + 8 \\).\n\nFrom the second equation, we can factor out \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n2xy = 4y + 8 \\implies 2x = 8 \\implies x = 4.\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting \\( x = 4 \\) into the first equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n4^2 - y^2 = 4(4) - 19 \\implies 16 - y^2 = 16 - 19 \\implies 16 - y^2 = -3 \\implies y^2 = 19.\n\\]\n\nSince \\( y^2 = 19 \\) has no integer solutions, we need to re-evaluate our approach considering the magnitude condition \\( |z| = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\) is an integer. Given \\( x = 3 \\), we check if \\( y = 4 \\) or \\( y = -4 \\) fits:\n\nIf \\( y = 4 \\):\n\n\\[\n|z| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer. Verifying the equation:\n\n\\[\nz = 3 + 4i,\n\\]\n\\[\nz^2 = (3 + 4i)^2 = 9 + 24i - 16 = -7 + 24i,\n\\]\n\\[\n4z - 19 + 8i = 4(3 + 4i) - 19 + 8i = 12 + 16i - 19 + 8i = -7 + 24i,\n\\]\n\nboth sides match. Therefore, \\( z = 3 + 4i \\) is a solution.\n\nIf \\( y = -4 \\):\n\n\\[\n|z| = \\sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5,\n\\]\nwhich is an integer. Verifying the equation:\n\n\\[\nz = 3 - 4i,\n\\]\n\\[\nz^2 = (3 - 4i)^2 = 9 - 24i - 16 = -7 - 24i,\n\\]\n\\[\n4z - 19 + 8i = 4(3 - 4i) - 19 + 8i = 12 - 16i - 19 + 8i = -7 - 8i,\n\\]\n\nboth sides do not match. Therefore, \\( z = 3 - 4i \\) is not a solution.\n\nThus, the only 