      "text": "To solve for the complex number \\( z \\), we start with the given equation \\( z^2 = 4z - 19 + 8i \\). Let's express \\( z \\) in the form \\( z = a + bi \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are real numbers. Substituting \\( z = a + bi \\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(a + bi)^2 = 4(a + bi) - 19 + 8i\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left-hand side, we have:\n\n\\[\n(a^2 + 2abi + (bi)^2) = a^2 - b^2 + 2abi = (a^2 - b^2) + 2abi\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\n4a + 4bi - 19 + 8i = (4a - 19) + (4b + 8)i\n\\]\n\nEquating the real and imaginary parts from both sides, we get the system of equations:\n\n\\[\na^2 - b^2 = 4a - 19 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2ab = 4b + 8 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nFrom equation (2), we can factor out \\( 2b \\) on the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n2ab - 4b = 8 \\implies 2b(a - 2) = 8 \\implies b(a - 2) = 4\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two cases: \\( b = 4 \\) and \\( a = 2 \\), or \\( a = 5 \\) and \\( b = 1 \\). We will consider each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: \\( b = 4 \\) and \\( a = 2 \\)**\n\nSubstituting \\( b = 4 \\) into equation (1):\n\n\\[\na^2 - 4^2 = 4a - 19 \\implies a^2 - 16 = 4a - 19 \\implies a^2 - 4a + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nFactoring the quadratic equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(a - 1)(a - 3) = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( a = 1 \\) or \\( a = 3 \\). Since \\( a = 2 \\) is already assumed, we have \\( a = 1 \\) or \\( a = 3 \\). This gives us the solutions \\( z = 1 + 4i \\) and \\( z = 3 + 4i \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( a = 5 \\) and \\( b = 1 \\)**\n\nSubstituting \\( a = 5 \\) into equation (1):\n\n\\[\n5^2 - 1^2 = 4 \\cdot 5 - 19 \\implies 25 - 1 = 20 - 19 \\implies 24 \\neq 1\n\\]\n\nThis case does not provide a valid solution for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) that satisfies both original equations.\n\nThus, the solutions are \\( z = 1 + 4i \\) and \\( z = 3 + 4i \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1 + 4i \\text{ or } 3 + 4i}\n\\]",
