      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The equation $z^2 - 4z + 19 - 8i = 0$ and $z^2 - 4z + 19 + 8i = 0$ are the factors given by the sum of a complex number and it's conjugate. The product of the two factors is the given complex number. Expanding, $z^2 - 4z + 19 - 8i = (z - (3 - 4i))(z - (3 + 4i)) = z^2 - 6z + 25$. This yields the equations $z^2 - 6z + 25 = 0$ and $z^2 - 6z + 19 = 0$.\n\nBecause 19 is not a quadratic residue, it doesn't yield factor pairs of 19 (which is square of an integer). Then the non-square roots don't yield factors (explicitly, a non-square, not equating to an imaginary unit squared, yields two non-square, non-conjugate roots, and thus non-factors). The discriminant of the above is $6^2 – 4 \\cdot 25 = 1$, an integer. It follows that the factors are $z - 3 + 4i$ and $z - 3 – 4i$. Then $z = (z-3 + 4i) + (z-3 – 4i) = 4(z-3) + (i - (i)) = \\boxed{4(0) + i = i}$. The answer is i",
